## QUESTION: KYA HAR READ REQUEST REPLICA SE AAYEGI?
-------------------------------------------------------

Nahi.

Read request ko hamesha Read Replica se serve karna zaroori nahi hai.

Application ye decide kar sakti hai ki particular read request ke liye
thoda stale data acceptable hai ya latest data chahiye.

Agar kisi read request ke liye thoda stale data acceptable hai,
to generally us request ko Read Replica se serve kiya ja sakta hai.
Isse read workload multiple replicas ke across distribute ho jaata hai

Agar kisi read request ke liye latest data immediately required hai,
to us request ko Primary DB se serve kiya ja sakta hai.

Aisa isliye kyunki Primary DB par data update hone ke baad,
us change ko Read Replicas tak pahunchne mein thoda time lag sakta hai.
Is duration mein Primary DB par latest data available hota hai,
lekin Read Replica par abhi bhi purana data present hota hai.

Isliye agar aisi read request ko us time Read Replica se serve kiya gaya,
to user ko stale ya outdated data mil sakta hai.

Example:

Generally Replica se serve kiye ja sakte hain
jab thoda stale data acceptable ho:

Product Listing
Product Details
Blog Posts
Public Feed


Primary DB se serve karna preferable ho sakta hai
jab latest data immediately required ho:

Account Balance
Payment Status
Critical Transaction Status



## READ REQUEST FLOW - LOCAL CACHE + REDIS + DB
--------------------------------------------------

1. Client se read request (GET) aati hai. 
   API Gateway request ke basis par usse relevant service ke Load Balancer ko route karta hai. 
   Service Load Balancer request ko us service ke kisi ek Instance (App 1, App 2, ... App N) tak forward karta hai.

2. Application Instance sabse pehle apne Local Cache mein requested data check karti hai.

3. Agar Local Cache Hit hota hai, to application directly Local Cache se data return kar deti hai.

4. Agar Local Cache Miss hota hai, to application Redis Cluster ke relevant Redis node mein requested data check karti hai.

5. Agar Redis Cache Hit hota hai, to Redis se data retrieve karke Application Instance ke Local Cache ko populate/update kiya jata hai, aur uske baad data client ko return kar diya jaata hai.

6. Agar Redis Cache Miss hota hai, to application available Read Replicas mein se kisi suitable replica ko request route karke wahan se data fetch kar sakti hai.

7. Read Replica se data retrieve hone ke baad application Redis Cache ko latest data se populate karti hai.

8. Application apne Local Cache ko bhi same data se populate/update karti hai.

9. Finally, application retrieved data ko client ko return kar deti hai.


## WRITE REQUEST FLOW - PRIMARY DB + REDIS AND APPLICATION LOCAL CACHE INVALIDATION
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Client se write request (POST / PUT / DELETE) aati hai, 
   API Gateway request ke basis par usse relevant service ke Load Balancer ko route karta hai. 
   Service Load Balancer request ko us service ke kisi ek Instance (App 1, App 2, ... App N) tak forward karta hai.

2. Application write operation ko Primary Database par execute karti hai.

3. Agar Primary DB write operation fail ho jaata hai, to application error response return kar deti hai. 

4. Agar Primary DB mein write successfully complete ho jaata hai, to corresponding data ke liye sabhi Application Instances ke Local Cache(s)
   aur Redis Cluster ke relevant Redis node mein stored cache entry ko invalidate ya update kiya jaata hai.

5. Cache invalidation/update complete hone ke baad application success response client ko return kar deti hai.


-------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: DATABASE REPLICATION KAISE HOTI HAI?
-------------------------------------------------------

Replication ka matlab hai:

Primary DB me hone wale data changes ko
Read Replica(s) tak propagate/apply karna.

## IMPORTANT
--------------

Replication ka matlab ye nahi hota ki har write ke baad
poora Primary DB dobara replicas me copy kiya jaata hai.

Poora database baar-baar copy karna extremely expensive hoga.

Isliye databases generally incremental changes replicate karte hain.

Matlab:

    Sirf Primary DB me hone wale naye changes
    replicas tak propagate kiye jaate hain.

Example — MySQL style:

1. Primary DB par write hota hai.

2. Primary DB change ko replication/change log me record karta hai.
   MySQL me ise Binary Log (Binlog) kehte hain.

3. Replica(s) Primary ke change log se changes receive/read karti hain.

4. Replica(s) un changes ko apne database me apply karti hain.


---------------------------------------
## QUESTION: REPLICATION LAG KYA HAI ?
---------------------------------------

Replication instant nahi hoti.

Primary DB par change hone aur Replica par
us change ke apply hone ke beech jo delay hota hai,
use Replication Lag kehte hain.

## EXAMPLE:
------------

User profile update:

    Old name: Anand
    New name: Anurag

Assume karo ki successful update ke baad
application ne corresponding Redis aur
application instances ke Local Cache entries
invalidate kar di hain.

Step 1:
    Primary DB par name successfully update ho gaya.

    Primary DB:
        name = Anurag

Step 2:
    Redis aur Local Cache me old value available nahi hai,
    isliye immediately updated name ke liye next read request
    Read Replica se serve ki jaati hai.

Step 3:
    Replica par abhi tak latest change apply nahi hua tha.

    Read Replica:
        name = Anand

Result:

    User ki request Read Replica se serve hoti hai
    aur user ko old name "Anand" milta hai.

    Yaani database ke Primary DB me data already updated hai,
    lekin replication lag ki wajah se user ki request par
    usse stale (outdated) data mil raha hai.

Is situation ko Stale Read kehte hain.


--------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: REPLICATION LAG KAISE HANDLE KARE ?
--------------------------------------------------

Common strategies:

#1. READ-YOUR-WRITE FROM PRIMARY DB
------------------------------------

    Agar user ne abhi data update kiya hai,
    to immediate next read primary DB se karao.

    Example:

        Update profile -> Primary DB
        Immediately fetch profile -> Primary DB
        Later reads -> Replica(s)

2. CRITICAL READS FROM PRIMARY DB
----------------------------------

    Important data primary database se read karo always.

    Example:

        Bank balance
        Payment status
        Order confirmation

    Jahan slight staleness acceptable ho,
    wahan reads Replica(s) se serve ki ja sakti hain.

    Example:

        Product listing
        Blog posts
        Catalog data
        Public feed


----------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: KYA REPLICATION EXPENSIVE HAI ?
----------------------------------------------

Haan, replication free nahi hoti.

Costs:

- Primary DB ko changes log karne padte hain
- Changes replicas tak network ke through jaate hain
- Replicas ko changes apply karne padte hain
- Additional storage required hota hai
- Replication lag ho sakta hai

Benefits:

- Primary DB se read load shift ho jaata hai
- System zyada read traffic handle kar sakta hai

So, replication ka cost hota hai, lekin read-heavy systems mein
ye cost often worth it hota hai.











